skip(2016,1,3,'Odd exponents are eliminated modulo 8; even exponents lie strictly between consecutive squares.')
skip(2016,2,2.5,'The two symmetric chords identify the circle centre as the circumcentre of ABC, giving the third chord symmetry.')
add(2016,3,4,'Algebra',['fractional parts','construction','bounds'],r'Write $\{x\}=x-\lfloor x\rfloor$. Prove that infinitely many positive reals satisfy $\{x^2\}-\{x\}>2015/2016$, but none lies below $1000$.',r'''For a sufficiently large integer $m$, take $x=m+1/(m+1)$. Directly reducing $x^2$ modulo one gives
$$\{x^2\}-\{x\}=1-\frac3{m+1}+\frac1{(m+1)^2},$$
which exceeds $2015/2016$ once $m+1>6048$.

Conversely write $x=a+b$ with integer $a\ge0$ and $0\le b<1$. The proposed inequality forces $b<1/2016$. If $x<1000$, then $a\le999$, and
$$\{x^2\}-b\le2ab+b^2-b<\frac{1998}{2016}+\frac1{2016^2}<\frac{2015}{2016}.$$
This contradiction proves the second claim.''','The construction must simultaneously control two fractional parts, and the lower-size obstruction needs a quantitative bound.')
add(2016,4,5,'Combinatorics',['binary strings','Fibonacci numbers','modular recurrence'],r'Let $B_n$ count binary strings of length $n$ in which every position has a neighbouring position equal to $1$. Find the smallest $n\ge2$ for which $20\mid B_n$.',r'''The answer is $25$. The endpoint condition forces the second and penultimate entries to be $1$; internally, two entries at distance two cannot both be zero. Splitting into odd and even positions therefore separates the restrictions.

Let $F_0=0,F_1=1,F_{j+2}=F_{j+1}+F_j$. Binary strings of length $r$ with no consecutive zeros are counted by $F_{r+2}$, by separating strings ending in $1$ and in $10$. Hence
$$B_{2m}=F_{m+1}^2,\qquad B_{2m+1}=F_mF_{m+3}.$$
(The odd formula for $m=1$ follows directly.) The Fibonacci residues give $2\mid F_j$ exactly when $3\mid j$, and $5\mid F_j$ exactly when $5\mid j$; these follow by checking the repeating pairs modulo $2$ and $5$.

For even lengths, divisibility by $20$ first occurs at $m+1=15$, giving $28$. For odd lengths, the two Fibonacci factors have the same parity, so divisibility by $4$ occurs exactly when $3\mid m$. Divisibility by $5$ also requires $5\mid m$ or $5\mid m+3$. The smallest positive $m$ meeting both requirements is $12$, giving $n=25$.''','A parity-position decomposition uncovers two Fibonacci factors; modular periodicity is then needed for the exact first index.')
skip(2016,5,3,'Subtracting pairs of equations forces either equal variables or a variable equal to -2, leaving quadratic cases.')
skip(2016,6,3,'Remove common powers of two, then the odd factor in the sum of cubes must be one.')
add(2016,7,5,'Combinatorics',['plane colouring','distance graphs','contradiction'],r'Every point in the plane receives one of four colours. Prove that two equally coloured points are separated by either $1$ or $\sqrt3$.',r'''Assume no such pair exists. Take a unit regular hexagon and its centre. The centre and any three consecutive vertices are pairwise at distances $1$ or $\sqrt3$, so they use all four colours. Applying this to overlapping triples forces opposite vertices of the hexagon to have the same colour.

Any two points at distance $2$ can be opposite vertices of such a hexagon. Thus every point on the circle of radius $2$ about a fixed point has that fixed point's colour. Two points on this circle can be chosen at distance $1$, contradicting the assumption.''','A rigid four-colouring of a small hexagonal configuration forces a global distance-two invariant and then a contradiction.')
add(2016,8,7,'Geometry',['construction','cyclic hexagons','area inequalities'],r'Three distinct concurrent lines meet at $P$. Prove that a circle containing $P$ can meet the lines at cyclically ordered points $A,B,C,D,E,F$ with $AB=CD=EF$. Prove also that every such circle satisfies $2([APB]+[CPD]+[EPF])\le[ABCDEF]$.',None,'A non-obvious tangent construction establishes existence; six similar triangles then convert the area claim into a symmetric fourth-power inequality.')
skip(2017,1,3.5,'Small constructions and an endpoint-preserving induction handle every n at least four.')
add(2017,2,4.5,'Number Theory',['divisibility','valuations','pigeonhole principle'],r'For five distinct integers, multiply their ten positive pairwise differences. Find the largest integer that divides this product for every choice of the five integers.',r'''The answer is $288=2^5\cdot3^2$. The choice $0,1,2,3,4$ gives exactly $288$, so it remains to prove universal divisibility.

At least three numbers have the same parity. Their three differences are even, and one is divisible by four; they contribute at least four factors of two. Of the other two numbers, either their difference is even, or one has the parity of the first triple and supplies a further even difference. Thus $2^5$ divides the product.

Modulo three, either some residue class contains three numbers, or two residue classes each contain two. In either case at least two pairwise differences are divisible by three. Hence $3^2$ divides the product as well.''','Separate sharp lower bounds for the 2-adic and 3-adic valuations require nested pigeonhole arguments, followed by an equality example.')
skip(2017,3,3.5,'Setting y=0 makes the function constant on a half-line; one further substitution extends this to all real numbers.')
add(2017,4,6,'Geometry',['extremal principle','finite point sets','circumcircles'],r'A set $S$ consists of $2017$ points, not all collinear. Prove that some triangle with vertices in $S$ has its circumcentre outside $S$.',r'''Choose a closest pair $A,B\in S$. The open disk with diameter $AB$ contains no point of $S$, since any interior point would be closer to $A$ than $B$ is.

Move the centre of this circle continuously along the perpendicular bisector of $AB$, keeping the circle through $A,B$, towards a half-plane containing a point of $S$ off line $AB$. The disks eventually include every fixed point in that half-plane. Stop at the first time a third point $C\in S$ reaches the circumference. No point of $S$ lies in the open disk at that moment, by the first-contact choice. The centre of this nondegenerate circle is in its open disk and therefore is not in $S$. It is the circumcentre of triangle $ABC$.''','An extremal empty-circle construction is needed; the finiteness and first-contact argument must be justified carefully.')
skip(2017,5,3,'Grouping terms between consecutive squares makes every completed block contribute exactly one.')
add(2017,6,5.5,'Geometry',['inversion','tangents','parallelograms'],r'Circles $K_1,K_2$ meet at $A,M$. The tangent to $K_1$ at $A$ meets $K_2$ again at $B$, and the tangent to $K_2$ at $A$ meets $K_1$ again at $D$. Reflect $A$ in $M$ to obtain $C$. Prove that $ABCD$ is cyclic.',r'''Invert about $A$ with radius $AM$, using primes for images. The point $M$ stays fixed. Circle $K_1$ becomes line $D'M$, parallel to its tangent $AB$; similarly $K_2$ becomes line $B'M$, parallel to $AD$. Hence $AB'MD'$ is a parallelogram.

Since $AC=2AM$, the image $C'$ is the midpoint of $AM$. This diagonal midpoint is also the midpoint of $B'D'$, so $B',C',D'$ are collinear. Inverting this line back gives the circle through $A,B,C,D$.''','The midpoint condition becomes a diagonal intersection after inversion, turning the two tangent circles into a parallelogram.')
add(2017,7,4.5,'Combinatorics',['matchings','cycles','gcd'],r'A thousand athletes stand one metre apart around a circular track of length $1000$ metres. Count partitions into pairs when each pair must be separated around the track by (a) $335$ metres; (b) $336$ metres.',r'''Label the athletes modulo $1000$, and join labels differing by $k$. This graph consists of $\gcd(1000,k)$ disjoint cycles, each of length $1000/\gcd(1000,k)$. A pairing is a perfect matching of these cycles. Each even cycle has exactly two perfect matchings, obtained by taking alternating edges, while an odd cycle has none.

For $k=335$ there are five cycles of length $200$, giving $2^5=32$ pairings. For $k=336$ there are eight cycles of length $125$, so no pairing exists.''','The modular-distance condition must be decomposed into cycles; parity then determines the number of perfect matchings.')
add(2017,8,4.5,'Number Theory',['quadratic congruences','root symmetry','prime moduli'],r'Put $f(x)=x^2-45x+2$. Find all integers $n\ge2$ for which precisely one of $f(1),\ldots,f(n)$ is divisible by $n$.',r'''If $a$ is the unique root modulo $n$, then $45-a$ is another root, since $f(45-a)=f(a)$. Uniqueness gives $2a\equiv45\pmod n$. But
$$4f(a)=(2a-45)^2-2017,$$
so $n\mid2017$. The number $2017$ is prime (trial division by the primes at most $43$ suffices), and therefore $n=2017$.

Conversely modulo $2017$, the equation is $(2a-45)^2\equiv0$, which has exactly the one solution $a\equiv1031$. Thus $n=2017$ works.''','Uniqueness combines with the reflection symmetry of a quadratic to force its discriminant to vanish modulo n.')
skip(2018,1,2.5,'Reduction modulo 16 leaves only small factorials to check.')
add(2018,2,4,'Combinatorics',['ternary representation','progression-free sets','construction'],r'Among $(3^{100}+1)/2$ equally spaced points on a line, choose $2^{100}$ so that none is the midpoint of two other chosen points.',r'''Number the points $0,1,\ldots,(3^{100}-1)/2$. Select the numbers having only $0$ and $1$ in their hundred-place ternary representation; there are exactly $2^{100}$, all within the range.

If selected numbers satisfied $a+c=2b$, reduce modulo $3$. The last ternary digits must all agree: their values lie in $\{0,1\}$ and the only possibilities for $a_0+c_0=2b_0\pmod3$ are all zero or all one. Remove this common digit and divide by three. Repeating shows every digit agrees, so $a=b=c$. Thus no three distinct selected points form an arithmetic progression.''','A base-three digit construction prevents carries and gives the required large progression-free set.')
skip(2018,3,3,'The three specified chords are simply the internal angle bisectors of triangle ACG.')
add(2018,4,5,'Algebra',['functional equations','surjectivity','injectivity'],r'Find all functions $f:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $f(xy+f(y))=y f(x)$ for every real $x,y$.',r'''The solutions are $f\equiv0$ and $f(x)=1-x$. Both satisfy the equation directly.

For any other possibility, choose $a$ with $f(a)\ne0$. Keeping $x=a$ and varying $y$ shows that $f$ is surjective. Setting $x=0$ gives $f(f(y))=y f(0)$. Surjectivity of the left side forces $f(0)\ne0$, so this identity also implies that $f$ is injective.

At $y=1$ the identity gives $f(f(1))=f(0)$, hence $f(1)=0$. Finally the original equation with $x=1$ gives $f(y+f(y))=0=f(1)$. Injectivity yields $y+f(y)=1$, as claimed.''','The nonzero branch must first be shown surjective and then injective before simple substitutions determine the function.')
skip(2018,5,2.5,'A one-step relation between successive partial sums supplies the second factor of n.')
skip(2018,6,3.5,'Equal chords in the two circles provide the two directed-angle equalities immediately.')
add(2018,7,4.5,'Number Theory',['divisor function','descent','prime factorization'],r'Let $b_1>1$ be an integer and define $b_{n+1}=d(b_n)^2$, where $d(m)$ is the number of positive divisors of $m$. Prove that some term equals $9$.',r'''Every term from $b_2$ onward is a square; a square has an odd number of divisors, so every term from $b_3$ onward is an odd square greater than one.

For an odd square $m=\prod p_i^{2e_i}$, its next term is $\prod(2e_i+1)^2$. For every odd prime $p$ and positive integer $e$, the elementary induction $p^e\ge3^e\ge2e+1$ holds, with equality only for $p=3,e=1$. Therefore $d(m)^2\le m$, with equality only when $m=9$. (Equality cannot involve two different primes, both equal to $3$.)

The positive terms thus strictly decrease from index three until reaching $9$, where they remain. Infinite strict descent is impossible.''','After isolating odd squares, a factor-by-factor divisor bound gives strict descent with one exceptional fixed point.')
add(2018,8,4.5,'Combinatorics',['subset sums','pigeonhole principle','extremal construction'],r'Rocks have positive integer masses totalling $2018$ kilograms, and cannot be split into two piles of $1009$ kilograms. What is the largest possible number of rocks?',r'''The answer is $1009$, attained by $1009$ rocks of mass $2$, since no subset then has odd total mass.

Suppose there were $m>1009$ rocks. List them in any order and take the $m$ partial sums before each rock, starting with $0$, regarded modulo $2018$. They are distinct because the masses are positive and their total is $2018$. This set $S$ and its translate $S+1009$ have total size greater than $2018$, so they intersect. Two partial sums therefore differ by $1009$ modulo $2018$, and, lying between $0$ and $2017$, differ in absolute value by exactly $1009$. The intervening block of rocks is the forbidden half-mass pile.''','A sharp example is easy, but the upper bound requires applying pigeonhole to partial sums and their half-total translate.')
skip(2020,1,3,'Squaring reduces the equation to consecutive doubled squares, with a short integrality check.')
add(2020,2,4,'Combinatorics',['games','modular strategy','invariants'],r'Three piles initially contain $2020$ stones each. A move chooses one pile, discards all others, and splits the chosen pile into two or three nonempty piles. Players alternate, and the player unable to move loses. Prove that the second player can force a win.',r'''The second player always leaves every pile with size congruent to $1$ modulo $3$. This holds initially. Splitting such a pile into two or three parts cannot leave every part congruent to $1$, since their sum would then be $2$ or $0$ modulo $3$.

The second player chooses a part of size $s\not\equiv1\pmod3$. If $s\equiv2$, split it into $1,s-1$; if $s\equiv0$, split it into $1,1,s-2$. Each new pile is positive and congruent to $1$. Each second-player reply is legal. Since the total number of stones decreases whenever piles are discarded and no infinite sequence of proper splittings is possible, play ends with the first player unable to move.''','Winning positions are characterized by a modular invariant on every remaining pile; the reply must handle both other residue classes.')
add(2020,3,4.5,'Geometry',['coordinates','tangents','right triangles'],r'Triangle $ABC$ is right-angled at $C$. Its circumcircle tangent at $C$ meets line $AB$ at $D$. Let $E$ be the midpoint of $CD$ and choose $F\in EB$ with $AF\parallel CD$. Prove $CF\perp AB$.',r'''Use coordinates $A=(-1,0),B=(1,0),C=(u,v)$ with $u^2+v^2=1$. The existence of $D$ gives $u\ne0$, and nondegeneracy gives $v\ne0$. The tangent is $ux+vy=1$, so
$$D=(1/u,0),\qquad E=((u+1/u)/2,v/2).$$
The point $G=(u,uv/(u-1))$ lies on $BE$, as its parameter along $B+t(E-B)$ is $t=2u/(u-1)$. Moreover
$$C\cdot(G-A)=u(u+1)+\frac{uv^2}{u-1}=0,$$
using $v^2=1-u^2$. Therefore $AG$ is parallel to the tangent $CD$, so $G=F$. Since $F$ and $C$ have the same first coordinate, $CF$ is perpendicular to $AB$.''','A tangent-midpoint construction requires a coordinated use of the circle equation and line intersections.')
add(2020,4,5.5,'Algebra',['recurrences','Pell equations','algebraic numbers'],r'Let $A_1=B_1=1$, $A_{n+1}=(A_n+2)/(A_n+1)$, and $B_{n+1}=(B_n^2+2)/(2B_n)$. Prove that $B_{n+1}=A_{2^n}$ for every $n\ge0$.',r'''Define integers $p_m,q_m$ by $(1+\sqrt2)^m=p_m+q_m\sqrt2$. Multiplying by $1+\sqrt2$ gives
$$p_{m+1}=p_m+2q_m,\qquad q_{m+1}=p_m+q_m.$$
Consequently the ratios $p_m/q_m$ start at $1$ and satisfy the recurrence for $A_m$, so $A_m=p_m/q_m$.

Squaring the same algebraic number instead gives
$$p_{2m}=p_m^2+2q_m^2,\qquad q_{2m}=2p_mq_m.$$
Thus $A_{2m}=(A_m^2+2)/(2A_m)$. Starting at $m=1$ and repeatedly doubling the index produces exactly the recurrence for $B$, proving $B_{n+1}=A_{2^n}$.''','The two apparently unrelated recurrences become multiplication and squaring of the same quadratic algebraic number.')
add(2020,5,4.5,'Combinatorics',['binary sequences','forbidden patterns','periodicity'],r'A binary sequence satisfies $a_n+a_{n+1}\ne a_{n+2}+a_{n+3}$ and $a_n+a_{n+1}+a_{n+2}\ne a_{n+3}+a_{n+4}+a_{n+5}$ for all $n\ge1$. Prove that $a_1=0$ implies $a_{2020}=1$.',r'''Call the two restrictions A and B. Four equal consecutive digits violate A. A block $010$ would force the next three digits to be $001$ by A, creating $010001$, which violates B. Complementing all digits also rules out $101$. Finally $0110$ and $1001$ violate A directly.

These observations say that every complete run has length neither one, two, nor at least four; hence each has length exactly three. The initial run of zeros has length one, two, or three. Thereafter the pattern repeats $111000$. Since $2020\equiv4\pmod6$, its digit is $1$ in each of those three cases.''','Short forbidden blocks force a global period; all initial phases must then be checked.')
add(2020,6,4.5,'Geometry',['power of a point','coordinates','loci'],r'Inside square $ABCD$, choose $P$. Two perpendicular lines through $P$ meet opposite sides $AB,CD$ at $W,Y$ and $BC,DA$ at $X,Z$, respectively. Determine the points $P$ for which $WXYZ$ is cyclic for every such pair of lines.',r'''Exactly the points on either diagonal work. Take the unit square with $P=(x,y)$. For a unit direction vector $(s,t)$ along $WY$, the perpendicular direction along $XZ$ is $(-t,s)$. Intersections with the horizontal sides give
$$PW\cdot PY=\frac{y(1-y)}{t^2},$$
while intersections with the vertical sides give
$$PX\cdot PZ=\frac{x(1-x)}{t^2}.$$
The four points are cyclic precisely when these products agree. Thus the condition is independent of the choice of valid directions and equivalent to
$$y(1-y)=x(1-x),\qquad (x-y)(1-x-y)=0.$$
These are exactly the two diagonals. Axis-parallel lines are valid for every interior $P$, so necessity is also established.''','The universal direction quantifier disappears after expressing cyclicity by equal chord products.')
add(2020,7,6,'Combinatorics',['tilings','Fibonacci numbers','recurrences'],r'Let $T_n$ count tilings of a $2\times2n$ rectangle by arbitrary tetrominoes (connected unions of four unit squares). Prove that $T_n$ is a perfect square for every positive $n$.',None,'A careful classification of the left boundary yields a recurrence involving partial sums; identifying it with a Fibonacci square completes the proof.')
add(2020,8,6,'Number Theory',['construction','Chinese remainder theorem','perfect powers'],r'For each $2\le k\le100$, prove that positive integers $b_2,\ldots,b_{101}$ can satisfy $b_2^2+b_3^3+\cdots+b_k^k=b_{k+1}^{k+1}+\cdots+b_{101}^{101}$.',r'''Choose $a_2=11$ and $a_i=1$ for $3\le i\le100$. The signed difference of the first hundred powers is
$$C=\sum_{i=2}^k a_i^i-\sum_{i=k+1}^{100}a_i^i=121+(k-2)-(100-k)=2k+19>0.$$
Let $L=\operatorname{lcm}(2,3,\ldots,100)$. Since $101$ is prime, it is coprime to $L$, so some positive multiple $D$ of $L$ satisfies $D\equiv-1\pmod{101}$.

Set $b_i=a_iC^{D/i}$ for $2\le i\le100$ and $b_{101}=C^{(D+1)/101}$. All exponents are positive integers. The desired signed difference among indices at most $100$ is now $C^D C=C^{D+1}=b_{101}^{101}$, as required.''','The construction needs a common scaling exponent that simultaneously preserves all lower powers and turns their difference into a 101st power.')
